Funambule-Noir

Ingredients: TRIP HOP + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Acoustic Guitar
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a wedding between two rival circus dynasties.

Funambule-Noir sounds like a phantom carny band playing a down-tempo wake inside an abandoned big top, where suffocating, pitch-shifted trip-hop breakbeats collide with bone-dry, microtonal acoustic guitar fingerpicking. The music breathes with wet, resonant sub-bass, phantom vinyl hiss made from compressed sawdust, and spectral, operatic vocal fragments that sound like they were recorded inside an iron lung.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 62–74 BPM. Dragging, drunk-swung "limp-stride" hip-hop grooves coupled with elastic, off-beat acoustic guitar strumming that feels like a trapdoor opening beneath the tempo.
- Harmonic Palette: Hungarian minor and Phrygian dominant scales played in unstable microtonal tunings (specifically open D tuned down to 415Hz), spiked with sudden, unresolved augmented chords that sound like rotting wood bending under weight.
- Production Signatures: "Calliope-Gate" (sidechaining heavy sub-bass to the wheezing air-release of an ancient steam organ), extreme tape-flutter applied exclusively to nylon acoustic guitar leads to mimic heat shimmer, and binaural ghost-room reverb IRs captured inside abandoned wooden circus tents.
- Dynamics: Suspended, whisper-quiet tension built on solitary classical guitar arpeggios that violently detonate into suffocating, sub-heavy breakbeat drops laden with reversed brass and phantom crowd groans.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born under the blood-soaked canvas of a grand marquee in the Black Forest, Funambule-Noir emerged during the clandestine midnight nuptials of the House of Vane (high-wire aerialists) and the Belladonna Troupe (sideshow illusionists). To prevent a murderous feud, musicians from both dynasties jammed together over a broken gramophone playing 1990s Bristol hip-hop tapes, dragging nylon-string guitars across rusted calliope pipes while a ghost-light flickered. The resulting subculture—known as Le Cirque Crevé—drapes itself in moth-eaten velvet tailcoats, greasepaint smeared around blackened eyes, and heavy brass balance-poles worn as jewelry. Performers play in complete pitch darkness save for handheld stroboscopic lanterns, burning dried rosin and sawdust to create an asphyxiating, sweet-smelling smog while the audience sways in hypnotic, slow-motion paralysis.

Typical Instruments
- Guitare-Trapeze: A double-necked nylon acoustic guitar strung with rusted piano wire on the lower neck, played with brass thimbles and bows.
- Steam-Vented Akai MPC60: A vintage sampler modified with pneumatic air valves that release loud steam hissed on every snare strike.
- Phantom Calliope: A miniaturized 1890s pipe organ refitted with custom sub-bass synth oscillators.
- Glass-Harmonica Turntable: A record player with a quartz-crystal platter spinning battered wax discs of funeral dirges.

